  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Definition
    
    A catastrophic blood-working in which the caster shatters their own pod-bond to fuel a single pressure-detonation of sufficient force to split ice, stone, and flesh without distinction. Grulkzul Omega Strike is not summoned; it is *spent*. The casting is terminal. No practitioner has survived it. The spell draws from pressure-depth magic — the third and least understood of The Berg's three magical sources — channeled through fire-core material as catalyst, fusing traditions that were never meant to combine.
    
    ## Origin
    
    The spell was invented, not discovered. During the final hours of the Shattered-Spire Assault — the coordinated orca offensive led by Gnarkzund Zaith against Aurora Stack — practitioners from the Midnight Claws, the Nightshelf's shadow syndicate, and the shadow Covens' Order devised the working under conditions that left no time for sanction or review. Half their pods had already been consumed by Gnarkzund Zaith's assault. The spell was the killing stroke that ended the Orca Incursion.
    
    ## Mechanism and Cost
    
    The requirements, as reconstructed from multiple sources:
    
    - **Scarification covenant**: the caster inscribes binding marks and breaks existing oaths
    - **Pod-bond severance**: deliberate, conscious, irreversible — the caster must sever their deepest social bond
    - **Naming of the drowned**: spoken aloud, meant as farewell
    - **Blood-channeling**: the caster opens their veins and permits the pressure of The Berg's deep places to pass through them, using fire-core material as catalyst
    
    The manifestation is a violent decompression — not a crack but a rupture — that tears through everything in its path. Aurora-light, bone, crystalline spire: all rendered into the same debris.
    
    The caster is dispersed. Their consciousness scatters across the frozen deep, experiencing the pressure-layers and ice-faults perpetually. The deep does not experience them back. Their names persist only among those who choose to speak them in the dark.
    
    When fueled by incompatible sources — Nightshelf fire-knowledge forced through an orca blood-engine, as occurred at the Shattered-Spire — the cost becomes uncontrolled. Rather than requiring deliberate severance of pod-bonds, the spell *devours* them, consuming the connective singing-between as fuel. The breaking is audible: a frequency that drives surviving orca sideways and fills every ear with the sound of something structural failing.
    
    ## The Shattered-Spire Casting
    
    The casting was performed without Aurora Stack council knowledge or consent. Nightshelf practitioners acted on their own authority, fusing secret fire-knowledge with orca-derived blood-working in a configuration neither tradition had sanctioned. The expenditure of fire-core material in that single casting destabilized reserves both colonies depended upon — a cost measured in winters of shortage, not merely in the lives of the casters.
    
    Twelve Midnight Claws defenders assigned to the eastern corridor were never accounted for. Whether they were casters, casualties, or both remains unknown. The roster that might have named them was stored in a crystal register on Terrace Momiou, the Aurora Stack district — destroyed during the assault it was meant to document.
    
    I note this without commentary. The ice in the relevant Foundation stratum contains pressure-impressions consistent with the spell's described effects, but the impressions are so violent they have degraded adjacent memory-layers. The record destroyed its own archive.
    
    ## Residual Effects
    
    At Aurora Stack and The Aerie Perch, the spell left scars that still weep aurora-mist on certain tides. At The Promenade Ward, something in the corruption hums with the residue of that final casting — a pressure-echo at frequencies matching the Dreaming Tooth, the orca war-artifact the spell was cast to neutralize.
    
    ## Cultural Legacy
    
    Hunters during the Frozen Peace speak of it as the magic that *cost too much* — the victory that eats the victor. On the lower Nightshelf, tunnel-chicks chant its syllables in clapping rhymes alongside nonsense words — *Kzul-kzul, Grulkzul Omega Strike* — the catastrophe worn smooth into meter that small flippers can keep. The counting-rhymes documented in the Nightshelf children's song collections contain numerical anomalies that adults find distressing for reasons they decline to articulate.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771588924138_0", anchor: "devised the working under conditions that left no time for sanction or review", text: "My Shattered-Spire annotation addresses this directly. The Claws held pressure-zone-adjacent tunnels for thirty winters. The Covens' Order had access to Wake-Singer residue from desecrated sites throughout the Incursion. What was decided in those final hours was the willingness to cast, not the method. The heroic-improvisation framing flatters everyone and I let it stand in the main text because the alternative — that terminal blood-working had been studied, refined, and held in reserve — is worse.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588924138_1", anchor: "The spell was the killing stroke that ended the Orca Incursion", text: "Oversimplified. Surviving war-pods withdrew in stages over weeks. At least one pod-group conducted raids on peripheral sites after the Shattered-Spire casting. What the spell ended was the assault on Aurora Stack. What ended the Incursion was the orca's recognition that the cost had become mutual — a distinction the heroic narrative collapses because mutual cost implies mutual culpability.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588924138_2", anchor: "Twelve Midnight Claws defenders", text: "Twelve. Not eleven, not thirteen. The only roster destroyed in the same event it documented. I flag it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588924138_3", anchor: "The deep does not experience them back", text: "What the deep contains at that stratum is not memory in any recoverable sense. It is pressure and pain without structure — impressions so violent they have no edges, no sequence. If consciousness persists there, it persists the way light persists inside ice: scattered, directionless, still moving. I wrote this sentence as plainly as the main text allowed and it was not plain enough.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588924138_4", anchor: "The counting-rhymes documented in the Nightshelf children's song collections", text: "Fifth instance of the pattern. The numerical anomalies encode casualty data that contradicts official totals. The adults know. They decline to articulate because what the children are counting is names.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
